gned so they add to broad money, and the gap row is our calculation","note":"Broad money is the cash and bank deposits people and companies hold. Banks and the central bank create it when they lend or buy something, so every unit of it sits against something they own: claims on the rest of the world, loans to the government, and loans to companies and households. This book lists those sources month by month in the country's own currency, then takes off the part of the banks' own funding that is not money (their capital, and deposits, bonds and loans the country does not count as money), so the rows add up to broad money. Other items, net, is the survey's balancing line (provisions, payments on their way between banks, valuation differences), shown with the sign that makes the rows add up. Money and credit are two sides of the same balance sheet, so this is never added to a credit or debt figure. The IMF counts money the same way in every country, which can be wider than the country's own measure of money. The IMF's latest figure for the euro area is for May 2025. The lines the IMF publishes for the euro area leave up to 0.89% of broad money unplaced in the last two years, shown as its own row. Source: International Monetary Fund."}